<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muara Takus</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Muara Takus temple compound</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muara Takus is a Buddhist temple compound in Kampar Regency, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riau">Riau</a> province, on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. Within a walled enclosure stand several brick and sandstone stupas, the largest the Candi Mahligai, whose dating is debated. It is one of the main surviving monuments of the Buddhist culture associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a> and its successors in central Sumatra.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muara-takus.jpg" alt="Muara Takus" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anandajoti Bhikkhu from Sadao, Thailand · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:003_Site_from_South-West_(38244920665).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The complex is thought to have been built by the maritime <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya Empire</a> in the 11th century, and the architecture and design of its temples point clearly to a Mahayana Buddhist origin. The scholar Schnitger suggested that the main temples may have been extensively renovated in the 12th century. The area is thought to have served Srivijaya as both a religious and a trade centre. It is one of the largest and best-preserved ancient temple complexes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>.</p><p>The site was abandoned for many centuries until Cornet De Groot rediscovered it in 1860. W. P. Groenveld explored and surveyed it in 1880, and excavations have taken place from time to time since. Research in 1983 mapped the remains of an ancient embankment, the Mahligai temple compound and other structures. The site is now protected as a national monument.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>A stone wall about 1 metre high, 74 by 74 metres, surrounds the complex, entered by a gateway on the north side. Inside stand the remains of four substantial temples. Candi Mahligai is a lotus-shaped stupa on an octagonal base, 14.30 metres high, unique in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> though similar structures survive in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> and Myanmar; lion figures, barely visible from below, decorate its top level. East of it lies the base of Candi Palangka, built of red stone and now 1.45 metres high; its upper terraces, reportedly taller at the time of the first colonial expeditions, have collapsed.</p><p>Candi Bungsu, now 6.20 metres high, is built partly of red stone and partly of sandstone. Candi Tua has a base of 32.80 by 21.80 metres and rises 8.50 metres in terraces, recalling in its design the much larger stupa of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borobudur">Borobudur</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>. All the temples carry little decoration, the seated lions on the upper terraces being the most notable.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muara Takus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The temple complexes of Muaro Jambi and <b>Muara Takus</b> in Sumatra, and the Sailendra monuments of central Java, rank among the finest achievements of Asian religious art.</blockquote><a href="/empire/srivijaya-empire">Srivijaya Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
